Task-11: Configure Local identity provider in OpenShift

-> Create 5 users called user01 to user05
-> User password same as the user name
-> Htpasswd Provider name should be j2c-htpasswd
-> Htpasswd secret name should be j2c-htpasswd-secret

SOLUTIONS ::------------->>>>>>>>

Prerequisites
You must be logged in to the OpenShift cluster as a cluster administrator (e.g., kubeadmin).

You need access to a terminal with the oc CLI installed.

The htpasswd utility must be installed (typically available via the httpd-tools package on RHEL/CentOS) .

Step 1: Create the HTPasswd File
First, generate the flat file containing the user credentials. The -c flag creates a new file, -B uses bcrypt hashing (recommended), and -b allows you to supply the password in the command line .

Command:

bash
# Create the file with the first user (user01)
htpasswd -c -B -b users.htpasswd user01 user01

# Append the remaining users (remove the -c flag to avoid overwriting)
$$ htpasswd -B -b users.htpasswd user02 user02
$$ htpasswd -B -b users.htpasswd user03 user03
$$ htpasswd -B -b users.htpasswd user04 user04
$$ htpasswd -B -b users.htpasswd user05 user05
Comment: This creates a file named users.htpasswd where the passwords are hashed. Note that the password for each user is exactly the same as their username, as requested.

Step 2: Create the Secret in OpenShift
You must create a Secret in the openshift-config namespace that contains the htpasswd file. The key inside the secret must be named htpasswd .

Command:

bash
$$ oc create secret generic j2c-htpasswd-secret --from-file=htpasswd=users.htpasswd -n openshift-config

Comment: This command creates a secret named j2c-htpasswd-secret using the local file users.htpasswd. The --from-file flag maps the file to the key htpasswd inside the secret.

Step 3: Configure the OAuth Identity Provider
You need to update the cluster's OAuth configuration to add the HTPasswd provider. You can do this by editing the existing cluster OAuth object or applying a YAML file.

Option A: Using oc edit (Interactive)
Run the following command to open the OAuth configuration in your default editor:

bash
$$ oc edit oauth cluster
Under spec.identityProviders, add the following block:

yaml
apiVersion: config.openshift.io/v1
kind: OAuth
metadata:
  name: cluster
spec:
  identityProviders:
  - name: j2c-htpasswd       # The provider name as requested
    mappingMethod: claim
    type: HTPasswd
    htpasswd:
      fileData:
        name: j2c-htpasswd-secret  # The secret name as requested
Option B: Applying a YAML File
Alternatively, save the YAML above to a file (e.g., htpasswd-provider.yaml) and run:

bash
$$ oc apply -f htpasswd-provider.yaml
Comment: The name field sets the provider name to j2c-htpasswd. The fileData.name references the secret j2c-htpasswd-secret created in the previous step . The mappingMethod: claim is the standard setting for provisioning users.

Step 4: Verification
After applying the configuration, OpenShift will automatically restart the authentication pods (this may take a minute or two).

Check the OAuth Pods:

bash
$$ oc get pods -n openshift-authentication
Wait until the pods show Running status.

Verify the Identity Provider:

bash
$$ oc get oauth cluster -o yaml
Look for the 
j2c-htpasswd
 entry in the output.

Test Login:
Log out of the current session and try logging in as one of the new users:

bash
oc login -u user01 -p user01
You should see a successful login message 
